a new report in the medical journal the  lancet says there is a serious lack of  gender-specific research and data  when it comes to heart disease in women  heart disease as you may know is the  leading cause of death for  women globally the report recommends  more education on early detection for  health care providers  and the patients recruiting more women  for cardiac studies  and prioritizing research on heart  disease in women  cbs news senior medical correspondent  that's dr tara narula  met with a woman whose cardiac scared  during pregnancy  caught her by surprise  just weeks before her baby was due yulia  nurikan knew  something wasn't right i felt nauseous i  felt  dizzy and i felt like i was about to  face  concerned for the baby yulia rushed to a  local hospital in queens new york  where her daughter olivia was delivered  via emergency c-section  tests showed yulia had suffered an  aortic dissection  a potentially deadly tear in the lining  of the aorta  that affects blood flow to the body and  can potentially rupture the entire aorta  obviously if this happens then the  patient literally  dies instantly dr ismail el hamsi is an  aortic surgeon  at mount sinai hospital in manhattan  where yulia was taken for life-saving  surgery  leaving her newborn baby behind  after six days of only seeing olivia on  facetime calls  yulia finally got to hold her what did  it feel like to  hold her in your arms it felt like she  belonged there you say hi  had she not been pregnant julia says she  likely would have ignored her symptoms  and heart disease symptoms in women can  differ from those in men  rather than chest pain women sometimes  report indigestion  back pain or shortness of breath that's  one reason the authors of a new report  in the lancet say it is  critical to have more women's specific  studies  women are not small men we need to think  about women  as their own individual biology and  and come up with the answers for them  explain to us  how the current system is failing us  when it comes to research  and women we keep saying that we're  understudying  under recognizing under diagnosing these  women  and we need to make all kinds of efforts  to make sure that our  clinical trials have good representation  of women so when we have new devices new  drugs  the application is not just for men for  cbs this morning dr  tara narula new york
 

